  "audioBrief": {
    "script": "Welcome to your Sift Daily Briefing. Here is your clear, plain update on today's top stories.\n\nThe United States economy grew faster than expected in the third quarter of the year. Gross domestic product rose at a three point four percent annual rate last quarter. The Commerce Department released this advance estimate on Thursday. Economists surveyed by Reuters had expected a growth rate of three point two percent. Estimates from economists ranged from a one percent rate to four point two percent. Resilient consumer spending helped drive the higher overall growth rate. Business spending on artificial intelligence also boosted the national economy. These factors offset higher oil prices caused by conflict in the Middle East. Where things stand now, this official report provides the initial estimate for the quarter.\n\nTurning to foreign policy, the European Union and India are working to increase joint security cooperation. Both sides signed a formal Security and Defense Partnership in January of this year. They followed that agreement with their first formal security dialogue in March. They also held their fifth Maritime Security Dialogue in Brussels last month. Former Indian ambassador Mohan Kumar said Europe can be a credible defense partner. He noted that the conflict in Ukraine limits European focus and security resources. France remains India's strongest military partner, maintaining troops on Reunion Island and Mayotte. Germany, the largest economy in Europe, has also become a key defense partner for India. India still relies heavily on military equipment from Russia.\n\nIn business news, Microsoft announced plans on Thursday to raise twenty five billion dollars in bonds. The company holds a top tier AAA credit rating with S and P, a credit agency. Management targets one hundred seventy billion dollars in capital expenditures for 2026. This expansion aims to support artificial intelligence demand from clients like OpenAI and Anthropic. The bond offerings are scheduled to close on November fourth. Finance chief Amy Hood said Microsoft will access debt markets when conditions are attractive. The funds will cover working capital, equipment spending, stock repurchases, and debt repayment. Microsoft reported positive free cash flow, while Amazon, Meta, and Alphabet reported negative cash flow. Additionally, Oracle announced plans to raise ten billion dollars in new debt.\n\nNext, President Donald Trump declared foreign factory robot imports to be a national security threat. He issued an official presidential proclamation on October twenty-eighth. The decision could impact manufacturing at General Motors, Ford, Tesla, Boeing, and Lockheed Martin. Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick conducted an investigation into these imported industrial robots. The Commerce Department found risks to the industrial base and potential cybersecurity vulnerabilities. The proclamation orders US officials to negotiate new trade agreements within one hundred eighty days. Trump warned that he may impose tariffs, duties, or quotas if no agreements are reached. This action follows restrictions placed on robotic vacuums and humanoid robots in July 2026. Those earlier rules specifically exempted fixed industrial robots used in automotive factories.\n\nIn technology news, sensitive personal information from the Department of Homeland Security was leaked online. The exposed files contained official titles, phone numbers, email addresses, and office locations. A group called Distributed Denial of Secrets published data for over forty thousand personnel. A separate group called Department of Peace released records for over forty one thousand staff. The leaks unmasked ICE agents, operatives, contractors, and officials working in human intelligence and surveillance. Distributed Denial of Secrets claimed the leak was fair play due to government transparency issues. This breach follows recent data security incidents involving the Defense Department and the FBI. The Department of Homeland Security is currently working to secure its digital systems.\n\nFinally, India approved twenty two billion dollars in military spending. This decision comes amidst border tensions with China. American defense manufacturers may find substantial opportunities in India's expanding defense market. Israeli defense manufacturers may also find substantial opportunities in the expanding market. Russian defense manufacturers may likewise find substantial opportunities in India's expanding defense market.\n\nNext up, United States unemployment claims fall to their lowest level since July.\n\nU.S. initial jobless claims fell to 194,000 last week. This is the lowest level since mid-July. The U.S. Labor Department said claims declined from a revised 195,000 in the previous week. The four-week moving average eased to 196,750. Jobless claims have stayed below 200,000 for seven consecutive weeks. Employers have added an average of 68,000 jobs per month so far in 2026. Investors are now awaiting the October employment report due on November 6.\n\nTurning to international news, political tensions are mounting at the United Nations biodiversity talks. A French government delegate at the COP17 summit in Yerevan, Armenia, said four countries were frustrating progress. The delegate said Saudi Arabia, Russia, Argentina, and Brazil were trying to weaken scientific assessments by the IPBES biodiversity panel. A negotiator from another developed nation also said these four countries were leading this push. The pushback is directed at two scientific assessments on the overlap between nature and climate crises. The summit is due to end on October 30.\n\nIn technology news, Amazon\u2019s cloud-computing unit reported revenue of 45.98 billion dollars. This was up 39 percent year over year and above analyst projections. AWS operating income reached 17.77 billion dollars, up 55 percent. Amazon CEO Andy Jassy said core compute and storage revenue grew at their fastest rates in several years. Cloud revenue has also sped up at rivals Alphabet and Microsoft. During the quarter, AWS announced plans to expand its portfolio of Nvidia graphics processing units. AWS also acquired data analysis startup DuckLabs and signed deals with Meta and OpenAI.\n\nLooking at the economy, the U.S. economic expansion is in its seventh year, but many Americans feel miserable. Polls indicate they are stressed about the cost of living and anxious about job security. The consumer price index was 3.7 percent higher in September than a year earlier. Since the end of 2019, overall consumer prices are up by about 30 percent. Groceries are 32 percent more expensive and rents are up by more than a third. The Federal Reserve raised interest rates in September for the first time since 2023, but left them unchanged in October. The unemployment rate edged higher to 4.2 percent in September.\n\nIn other tech news, Intel shares fell more than 20 percent in late trading. Intel forecast fourth-quarter revenue of 15 billion dollars to 16 billion dollars, below analyst expectations of 17 billion dollars. A shortage of memory chips has pushed up prices and cut demand for laptops and desktops. Intel expects the PC market to shrink 12 percent this year and another 10 percent in 2027. Third-quarter revenue rose 21 percent to 16.5 billion dollars. Data centre and AI sales jumped 78 percent to 7.3 billion dollars. Intel recently sold 15 billion dollars in shares to fund its turnaround under chief executive Lip-Bu Tan.\n\nIn energy news, Sembcorp Utilities and DayOne Data Centers have signed a memorandum of understanding. The companies will explore integrated power and energy solutions for data centres in Asia. They will evaluate on-site power infrastructure, renewable energy supply, and operations and maintenance services. The collaboration falls under DayOne\u2019s Bring Your Own Power initiative. DayOne has an existing power purchase agreement with Sembcorp for its SG1 data centre in Singapore. DayOne targets 100 percent renewable energy by 2030.\n\nBefore we go, here is your thought for the day.\n\nTwo hundred thousand miles from Earth, the Apollo 13 crew faced carbon dioxide poisoning. Their damaged spacecraft had incompatible air filters. Every breath tightened their chest. On the ground, engineers dumped spacecraft junk onto a table. They used cardboard, plastic bags, and tape. Their impossible mission was to invent a functional air scrubber with just these items. Under extreme pressure, the team devised a square-peg-round-hole adapter. Transmitted verbally, the astronauts built it. It worked, buying them time for the journey home. This story shows how limits can spark fast human invention. What problem in your own life might look different if you used only what is already in front of you?\n\nThank you for listening to this part of the Sift Daily Briefing.",
    "audioUrl": "https://roshanroytk-ui.github.io/sift-data/daily_brief.mp3",
