  "audioBriefIndepth": {
    "script": "Welcome to your Sift In-Depth Briefing. This is our full analysis of today's big stories.\n\nHow fast is the American economy growing right now? Official data shows the United States economy grew by 3.4 percent over the summer. Gross domestic product measures the total value of all goods and services made in a country. For decades, the United States economy has relied heavily on ordinary people buying goods. Consumer spending makes up more than two-thirds of the entire American economy. When people spend money, businesses hire workers and build new factories. This cycle keeps the whole country growing over time. But when prices rise too fast, families struggle to pay for everyday items. The Federal Reserve is the central bank of the United States. It manages this balance by changing interest rates. Lower rates make borrowing cheap, which encourages spending and growth. Higher rates make borrowing expensive, which cools down inflation. Lately, energy costs have stayed high due to a conflict involving Iran. Average gas prices remain above four dollars a gallon in the United States. That is about one dollar more per gallon than one year ago. Higher gas costs usually force households to cut back on other spending.\n\nThe Commerce Department released new official numbers just before the midterm elections. These numbers showed the economy grew faster than most experts had expected. Gross domestic product grew at an annualized rate of 3.4 percent last quarter. That is an increase from the 2.2 percent growth rate recorded in the previous quarter. Economists polled by Reuters had forecast a 3.2 percent pace. Overall predictions ranged from a 1 percent rate to 4.2 percent. Consumer spending grew at a 3.8 percent rate during the quarter. Companies also spent heavily on artificial intelligence technology and gear. Meanwhile, a key inflation index preferred by the Federal Reserve rose to 3.6 percent in September. In August, that same inflation rate was 3.4 percent. Core inflation, which excludes volatile food and energy, reached 3.1 percent. A separate measure from the Labor Department reached 3.7 percent in September. Federal Reserve Chairman Kevin Warsh said economic activity is expanding strongly. The central bank kept its benchmark rate between 3.75 and 4 percent.\n\nTech companies borrowed huge sums over the past year for AI infrastructure. Low layoffs and moderate hiring also helped keep household spending surprisingly strong. Many voters still feel downbeat because of rising grocery and energy costs. Donald Trump has seen his economic approval ratings fall sharply in recent polls. Consumer sentiment is down 15 percent since February, according to Michigan survey data. Economists expect the Federal Reserve might raise rates in December if inflation stays high. Listeners should watch if tech firms reduce AI investments as borrowing costs remain elevated.\n\nNow let us turn to global security partnerships across the ocean.\n\nCan the European Union and India work together to keep the Indo-Pacific region safe? India and the European Union are expanding defense talks, but different security priorities create hurdles. The Indo-Pacific is a massive ocean region vital for world shipping and international trade. For decades, the United States maintained primary naval power across these trade routes. In recent years, China has expanded its military and naval influence across the region. India shares a border with China and considers China a major security rival. At the same time, India relies heavily on Russia for military equipment and hardware. Meanwhile, European countries faced a major security crisis when Russia invaded Ukraine in 2022. European leaders focused most of their military attention and money on protecting Europe. This focus limited how much attention European nations could pay to distant oceans.\n\nIn January, India and the European Union signed a formal Security and Defense Partnership. They followed that agreement with formal security dialogues throughout the year. Today's news is that the two sides held their fifth Maritime Security Dialogue in Brussels last month. Former Indian ambassador Mohan Kumar told DW that Europe can be a credible defense partner. He noted that France already cooperates with India using its naval bases on Reunion Island. France also maintains military assets on the nearby island of Mayotte. Diplomat Javed Ashraf said France stands out because it sells no weapons to China or Pakistan. However, former security official Tara Kartha said political differences limit the European Union as a whole. Leaders from the bloc met in Brussels recently and showed ongoing disagreements over military funding. European Studies professor Gulshan Sachdeva explained that India's Russian ties complicate defense industry integration.\n\nThe United States has pulled back slightly in the Eastern Hemisphere, opening room for new partnerships. India wants to build stronger military abilities without joining a strict military alliance. Germany, Spain, Italy, and Poland have all emerged as useful defense partners for India. For example, India has pursued submarine purchases from Germany and jet purchases from France. India also serves as a source of defense supplies for a re-arming Europe. Disagreements over how to handle China also make a common security outlook difficult to reach.\n\nThe new partnership creates a framework for sharing maritime data and developing technology together. Indian officials hope to access European drone technology tested during the war in Ukraine. However, the deal does not mean Europe will provide a joint military safety net for India. Watch whether European nations offer deeper industrial partnerships and chip technology to Indian defense firms.\n\nFinally, let us look at how technology giants are paying for artificial intelligence.\n\nHow are massive technology firms funding their giant artificial intelligence investments right now? Microsoft announced plans to sell 25 billion dollars in bonds to fund data centers. Artificial intelligence requires huge computer centers filled with thousands of specialized microchips. Building these data centers costs tens of billions of dollars in cash upfront. Companies must build facilities years before the new chips generate sales or profits. In the past, tech companies used cash from their regular business operations to pay for growth. Now, the required spending is so high that even wealthy firms are borrowing heavily. Amazon, Alphabet, and Meta have all spent historic sums to build out their networks. This heavy spending has pushed free cash flow into negative territory for several major firms.\n\nMicrosoft announced it will issue bonds to raise 25 billion dollars in new debt. The company is taking advantage of its top AAA credit rating from S and P. Microsoft aims for 170 billion dollars in capital spending for 2026. This spending supports demand from artificial intelligence labs like OpenAI and Anthropic. Finance chief Amy Hood said the bond offering is scheduled to close on November fourth. Unlike its competitors, Microsoft reported positive free cash flow during its latest earnings call. Alphabet reported negative free cash flow for the second quarter in a row. Amazon reported a negative free cash flow of 42.4 billion dollars over twelve months. Amazon spent 75.9 billion dollars on capital expenditures during the third quarter alone. Amazon's cloud unit grew revenue by 39.3 percent to 46 billion dollars. Amazon chief executive Andy Jassy pledged 220 billion dollars in spending for 2026. He added that Amazon expects to spend meaningfully more in 2027. Andy Jassy said AI server racks break even in less than three years. Amazon shares dropped roughly 6 percent in after-hours trading following below-expected earnings. Oracle also unveiled plans to raise 10 billion dollars in new debt.\n\nTech firms face immense pressure to secure server capacity before rivals claim market share. Analyst Sky Canaves noted that investors worry about high spending eating into company cash flows. Power shortages and construction delays have also held back new data center capacity. Business Insider reported that Amazon received billions in Nvidia server racks before deployment sites were ready.\n\nThese bond sales allow tech giants to expand networks without depleting cash reserves. However, construction delays could slow down when new server capacity turns into revenue. Watch whether rising interest rates make borrowing too expensive for long-term artificial intelligence investments.\n\nUp next, Donald Trump declares robot imports a national security threat.\n\nCould foreign factory robots pose a threat to American national security? President Donald Trump says foreign industrial robots now count as a security risk.\n\nFor many years, American factories installed automated machinery to build products faster and cheaper. Robots work on assembly lines and help workers complete heavy tasks safely. Automakers General Motors and Ford put heavy robot arms into their car assembly plants. Electric vehicle maker Tesla also built assembly lines with automated machinery. At the same time, aerospace giants like Boeing and Lockheed Martin used advanced imported equipment. Boeing and Lockheed Martin build airplanes and military weapon systems for the United States. Over time, American businesses relied more on foreign manufacturers for this heavy factory technology. But national leaders grew concerned about foreign control over vital manufacturing equipment. They worried about cybersecurity vulnerabilities in foreign computer software. Cybersecurity means protecting digital systems from unauthorized access or cyber attacks. In July 2026, the Federal Communications Commission took action. The agency banned foreign robot models like vacuum cleaners, four-legged robot dogs, and humanoid robots. However, that earlier action exempted fixed factory robot arms and warehouse machines.\n\nNow, the federal government is focusing on those heavy factory machines as well. Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick oversaw an official investigation into foreign robotics imports. His investigation reported that foreign machinery poses risks to the United States industrial base.\n\nOn October 28, President Donald Trump issued an official presidential proclamation. The proclamation describes industrial robots as vital tools for making military weapons. It calls robotics a driving force behind all critical infrastructure sectors in the country. The Department of Commerce investigation found unique security and safety risks in imported equipment. These issues could introduce significant cybersecurity vulnerabilities with severe consequences. Therefore, Trump ordered new negotiations with trading partners to adjust imports of robotics and machinery components. The government aims to strike new trade agreements within 180 days. If countries do not reach agreements within 180 days, the president warned he may take action. That action could include tariffs, duties, quotas, and extra fees on imported robots. A tariff is a tax placed on goods brought in from another country. A quota sets a strict limit on the number of imported items allowed.\n\nThe administration is pushing this plan now because officials want to safeguard military manufacturing lines right away. They worry that relying on foreign robot makers hurts long-term American competitiveness.\n\nThis move could complicate manufacturing operations for major corporations across the country. Companies might pay higher costs if the administration imposes tariffs on imported factory equipment. Next, we can watch if US officials reach trade deals within the 180-day window. If no agreements happen, new tariffs or quotas on foreign robots may go into effect.\n\nNow let us look at a major security issue affecting government workers in the United States.\n\nOver 41,000 US Department of Homeland Security employees had their data leaked in a security breach. Hackers exposed their information during this security incident. The data leak was conducted by Distributed Denial of Secrets and the Department of Peace.\n\nNext, we turn to defense news from across the globe in India.\n\nIndia approved 22 billion dollars in military spending. This decision comes amidst border tensions with China. American, Israeli, and Russian defense manufacturers are likely to find substantial opportunities. These opportunities exist within India's expanding defense market.\n\nUp next, US unemployment claims fall to the lowest level since July.\n\nAre fewer Americans losing their jobs right now? Yes, the number of people asking for government job loss benefits just fell to a new low.\n\nWhen companies let workers go, those workers apply for money from the government to help them live. This money is called unemployment benefits. The United States government counts these new applications every single week. If the number goes up, it usually means businesses are struggling and firing more people. If the number goes down, it means companies are keeping their workers. Over the past few years, the job market went through big ups and downs. By 2026, hiring slowed down quite a bit. Companies became careful about taking on new staff because costs were high. Yet, at the same time, companies did not want to fire the workers they already had. So worker firings stayed very low for many months.\n\nRecently, people worried that companies might start laying off more workers as the economy slowed down. Instead, new numbers show that even fewer people lost their jobs last week than before.\n\nThe United States Labor Department reported that initial jobless claims dropped to 194,000 last week. That is the lowest number since middle July. It fell from a revised 195,000 the week before. The four-week average eased to 196,750. Claims have stayed below 230,000 all year. They have been at or below 200,000 for seven weeks in a row. However, hiring remains quiet. Employers added an average of 68,000 jobs per month in 2026. Last month, the economy created only 29,000 jobs.\n\nThis is happening because people are still spending money on goods and services. Also, companies are investing a lot of money into artificial intelligence projects right now. These factors are helping the economy keep moving forward without big lay-offs.\n\nThis shows that the job market is staying strong even if hiring is slow. Next, investors are waiting for the October job report on November sixth. Economists surveyed by FactSet expect the economy added around 55,000 jobs that month. If hiring stays low, workers might find it harder to switch to new jobs. We will have to watch if companies keep holding on to their current teams.\n\nNow, let us turn to global nature talks happening in Europe.\n\nWhy are world leaders arguing over science at a big nature meeting? A few countries are trying to weaken scientific reports to protect their own business interests.\n\nFor years, countries have met at United Nations summits to protect world wildlife and plants. To make good rules, these countries rely on teams of scientists. One scientific group studies global warming, while another panel studies nature loss. These scientists write big reports showing how damaging air, water, and trees harms our planet. When nations agree on these reports, they use the facts to write global rules. However, some nations make a lot of money selling oil, gas, or farm goods. These nations often fear that environmental rules will hurt their profits. In the past, oil countries argued over words in climate reports. In 2025, a major United Nations environment report summary was completely stopped during country disagreements. Now, these arguments have spread to biodiversity talks about nature.\n\nAt the latest nature meeting, four countries began working together to block scientific findings. Diplomats say this creates a dangerous new trend for global environmental agreements.\n\nAt the COP17 summit in Yerevan, Armenia, four countries were accused of weakening science. Diplomats named Saudi Arabia, Russia, Argentina, and Brazil as the four nations. They are fighting over reports by the IPBES biodiversity panel. The disagreement is about changing words from welcome to take note of the facts. A French delegate said without science, discussions are just opinions. A negotiator from a developed country called the group a clear alliance working together. Bethan Laughlin from the Zoological Society of London said this sets a worrying precedent. IPBES chair David Obura declined to comment on the ongoing talks. The summit was set to end on October 30 without resolving the fight.\n\nThis fight is happening right now because nations are finalizing a new global deal for nature. Some fossil fuel and farming nations want to use scientific language as bargaining chips. They hope to avoid strict rules that could harm their key industries.\n\nThis fight means future environmental deals might end up with weaker targets. If scientific reports are not welcomed, countries may ignore environmental warnings. Next, we must watch if delegates can reach an agreement before leaving Armenia. We should also watch if similar deadlocks happen at future climate meetings like the IPCC.\n\nSwitching gears, let us look at the massive technology companies powering the internet.\n\nIs the business of powering the internet growing fast again? Yes, Amazon's cloud business just grew its fastest in years due to artificial intelligence demand.\n\nCloud computing means renting computer power and memory over the internet instead of buying big machines. Amazon created this industry when it launched Amazon Web Services, or AWS, in 2006. Companies use AWS to run websites, store files, and process big data. Later, tech giants Microsoft and Google built rival cloud networks called Azure and Google Cloud. For a long time, AWS stayed much bigger than its competitors. Recently, a new technology called artificial intelligence began changing the tech world. Building AI models requires giant computer facilities filled with powerful graphics chips. Tech companies started spending huge amounts of money to build these new data centers. Cloud providers rushed to offer these special AI tools to business customers.\n\nMany analysts expected cloud growth to stay quiet this year. Instead, sales exploded upward as businesses signed massive new deals to use artificial intelligence power.\n\nAmazon reported that AWS revenue grew 39 percent to 45.98 billion dollars. That beat analyst expectations of 45.29 billion dollars. It was the fastest growth for AWS since late 2021. AWS operating income jumped 55 percent to 17.77 billion dollars. The unit now makes up almost 23 percent of Amazon's total revenue. Meanwhile, Google Cloud revenue more than doubled. Microsoft said Azure is 72 percent as big as AWS, up from 58 percent two years ago. Amazon expanded its Nvidia chips, bought DuckLabs, and signed deals with Meta and OpenAI. Amazon will also sell its own Trainium AI chips to outside buyers starting in late 2027.\n\nThis surge is happening now because companies are racing to build AI tools. Businesses need huge amounts of computing power and data storage right away. So they are spending billions to rent capacity from major cloud providers.\n\nThis growth shows that corporate spending on artificial intelligence remains very strong. Amazon Chief Executive Andy Jassy predicted AWS could grow over 40 percent in 2027 and 2028. He thinks AWS could become a trillion-dollar business in five to ten years. However, rivals like Microsoft Azure continue to gain ground on Amazon. We should watch if cloud companies can build enough data centers to meet this demand.\n\nNext, we will explore why the American economy looks strong on paper but feels bad to many people.\n\nWhy does the US economy feel bad to so many people when official numbers look strong? High living costs and stagnant real wages have made many Americans feel financially stuck.\n\nThe American economic expansion is in its seventh year, with no recession in sight. Layoffs are low, and the economy is growing at its fastest pace in a year. People are spending money on travel, dining out, and live entertainment. Yet people faced the highest inflation in four decades right after the pandemic. Supply chains stretched and broke in twenty twenty-one. Russia's invasion of Ukraine choked off shipments of wheat, fertilizer, and oil. New trade tariffs raised prices on many goods. Massive spending on artificial intelligence pushed up software costs. Energy costs rose further during recent fighting involving Iran. Overall consumer prices have jumped about thirty percent since late twenty nineteen. Groceries cost thirty-two percent more now. Eating out costs forty percent more. Rents are up by more than a third. Electricity rose forty-three percent. Auto maintenance jumped over half, and car insurance rose forty-eight percent. Hourly pay rose about one-third, but rising prices erased those gains.\n\nThe situation changed because inflation slowed down from its peak of nine point one percent. But prices keep rising on top of past spikes.\n\nIn September, consumer prices were three point seven percent higher than a year earlier. Adjusted for inflation, pay fell year over year for six straight months through September. The unemployment rate rose slightly to four point two percent. Healthcare, construction, and manufacturing added jobs. But employers are not hiring many new workers. Glassdoor reported that employee confidence hit its lowest level in ten years. Entry-level college graduates are struggling, and long-term unemployment is growing. Home prices are up by about half since late twenty nineteen. Mortgage rates climbed above seven percent, up from under four percent. Excluding taxes and insurance, that doubles monthly payments for new buyers. Capital spending on data centres could reach five point five trillion dollars by twenty thirty, according to JPMorgan Chase. But a Reuters and Ipsos survey showed fifty-four percent of Democrats oppose new data centre construction. Thirty-seven percent of Republicans also oppose them. John Leer, chief economist at Morning Consult, said people worry economic rules are changing.\n\nSupply shocks are lasting longer and hitting harder. Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago president Austan Goolsbee warned about these frequent shocks in September. The Federal Reserve raised interest rates in September to tamp down inflation. It then left rates unchanged in October.\n\nEconomic anxiety keeps showing up as a top concern for voters ahead of midterm elections on November third. Watch whether job hiring picks up or if living costs continue to outpace paycheques.\n\nNow, let us turn to tech and global supply chains.\n\nWhy did shares in chipmaker Intel drop sharply in late trading? Intel issued a weak sales forecast due to memory chip shortages and data centre supply problems.\n\nIntel makes microchips that power personal computers and global data centres. Intel faced tough competition and delays in recent years. Chief executive Lip-Bu Tan took over to lead a major turnaround. Intel raised fifteen billion dollars by selling shares to fund new projects. Investors grew optimistic, and the stock price more than doubled this year. Computer makers need memory chips alongside main processors to build laptops and desktops. When memory chip prices surge, overall computer manufacturing slows down.\n\nThe situation changed when Intel released a sales forecast that missed Wall Street targets.\n\nBloomberg reported that Intel shares fell more than twenty percent in late trading. Intel expects fourth-quarter revenue between fifteen billion and sixteen billion dollars. Analysts expected seventeen billion dollars on average. Adjusted earnings are forecast at twenty-five cents a share, below estimates of forty-three cents. Third-quarter results were actually strong. Revenue rose twenty-one percent to sixteen point five billion dollars. Data centre and artificial intelligence sales jumped seventy-eight percent to seven point three billion dollars. But personal computer chip sales fell five percent to eight point one billion dollars. Intel expects the personal computer market to shrink twelve percent this year. It expects another ten percent drop in twenty twenty-seven. Research firm TrendForce reported in September that computer makers hit their budget limit for memory chips.\n\nChief executive Lip-Bu Tan predicted memory pricing will remain a severe issue in twenty twenty-seven. Tan added that data centre sales suffered from raw material shortages and a factory shutdown in Israel.\n\nThis setback shows how fragile high-tech supply chains remain. Watch to see if memory chip prices ease or if computer demand drops further in coming quarters.\n\nNext, we look at the massive energy needs of artificial intelligence in Asia.\n\nHow are tech companies planning to feed the energy appetite of modern data centres? Two companies signed an agreement to explore clean energy and power infrastructure for Asian data centres.\n\nData centres are facilities packed with computers running internet services and artificial intelligence. These facilities require vast amounts of constant electrical power. Sembcorp Utilities is a major energy provider based in Singapore. DayOne Data Centers is a Singapore-headquartered firm building digital infrastructure across Asia. Fast technology growth puts heavy strains on local power grids across the region. DayOne created an initiative called Bring Your Own Power. This program plans power supplies at the earliest stages of data centre development. It helps secure land, water, and power early in constrained markets. DayOne aims to run on one hundred percent renewable energy by twenty thirty.\n\nSembcorp Utilities and DayOne signed a memorandum of understanding to work together across Asia.\n\nDayOne announced the pact on Thursday. The companies will evaluate on-site power generation, electrical infrastructure, fuel storage, and renewable energy supply. DayOne chief executive Jamie Khoo said securing critical resources early allows fast execution. Sembcorp executive Koh Chiap Khiong said energy infrastructure must grow alongside digital infrastructure. DayOne already has a power purchase agreement with Sembcorp in Singapore for its upcoming SG1 facility. That project will run entirely on renewable energy. In June, DayOne signed deals in Malaysia under the Corporate Renewable Energy Supply Scheme. Those agreements cover one point five gigawatts of peak solar capacity. They also include two point two gigawatt-hours of battery energy storage. In Thailand, DayOne signed an agreement for its Chonburi Tech Park data centre campus.\n\nArtificial intelligence applications are expanding rapidly, driving an unprecedented surge in electricity demand.\n\nThis partnership aims to build stable energy foundations for digital growth. Watch to see if these feasibility studies lead to binding construction contracts for new power facilities.\n\nBefore we go, here is your thought for the day.\n\nTwo hundred thousand miles from Earth, the crew of Apollo thirteen faced severe danger. Their damaged spacecraft was running out of clean air. Carbon dioxide was building up inside the cabin. Every breath made the astronauts feel tighter in the chest.\n\nThe spacecraft had carbon dioxide filters, but they were incompatible. The round filters from one module did not fit the square slots of the other.\n\nDown on Earth, mission engineers rushed to solve the problem. They gathered items present on the spacecraft and dumped them on a table. The pile included cardboard, plastic bags, and tape.\n\nUnder extreme pressure, the team worked out a solution. They designed a crude adapter to fit a square peg into a round hole.\n\nEngineers read the instructions aloud over the radio link. Step by step, the astronauts built the adapter using their limited trash.\n\nIt worked. The homemade air scrubber pulled carbon dioxide from the air. That clever fix bought the crew enough time to journey safely back home.\n\nWhen pushed to the absolute edge, humans find creative ways to survive. Necessity drives incredible innovation.\n\nWhat unexpected resources do you have right now that could solve a tough problem in your life?\n\nThank you for spending time with Sift In-Depth today. We look forward to bringing you clear stories again next time.",
