  "audioBrief": {
    "script": "Welcome to your Sift Daily Briefing. Here is today's news delivered in clear, plain language.\n\nThe European Union wants a larger security role in the Indo-Pacific region. India is seeking new allies and building closer defense ties with European nations. In January 2026, India and the European Union signed a new defense partnership. They held a security dialogue in March and a maritime meeting last month. Former ambassador Mohan Kumar said Europe can be a credible defense partner for India. He added that the conflict in Ukraine limits European expansion in the region. France already cooperates with India using its military bases on Reunion Island and Mayotte. Former official Tara Kartha said Europe currently has very little regional security role. India still relies heavily on military equipment from Russia.\n\nNext, in economic news, the United States economy grew faster than expected last quarter. Gross domestic product measures the total output of goods and services. It increased at a 3.4 percent rate, according to the Commerce Department. The Bureau of Economic Analysis released this advance third-quarter estimate on Thursday. Strong consumer spending and business investment in artificial intelligence drove the growth. That spending offset higher oil prices caused by conflict in the Middle East. Economists polled by Reuters had forecast a 3.2 percent growth pace. Their individual estimates ranged from 1 percent to 4.2 percent. The strong data contrasts with household concern shown in low approval ratings for Donald Trump.\n\nTurning to technology, Amazon's cloud computing business reported faster revenue growth than expected last quarter. Amazon Web Services generated 45.98 billion dollars in third-quarter revenue. That total represents a 39 percent increase from the previous year. Analysts polled by StreetAccount had expected revenue of 45.29 billion dollars. Operating income for the unit rose 55 percent to 17.77 billion dollars. The division now accounts for almost 23 percent of Amazon's total revenue. Chief Executive Officer Andy Jassy said core compute and storage grew at their fastest rates in years. Amazon also acquired data analysis startup DuckLabs and signed deals with Meta and OpenAI. Rival Google Cloud reported that its revenue more than doubled during the same quarter.\n\nIn government policy news, President Donald Trump declared foreign factory robot imports a national security threat. He issued the presidential proclamation on October 28. An investigation by Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick found that foreign industrial robots pose cybersecurity risks. The directive orders new negotiations with trade partners to reach an agreement within 180 days. Trump warned that he may impose tariffs or quotas if no deal is reached. The scrutiny could affect major companies like General Motors, Ford, Tesla, Boeing, and Lockheed Martin. This action follows a July 2026 ban by federal regulators on foreign robotic vacuums and humanoid robots. That previous ban had exempted fixed industrial robot arms used in manufacturing factories.\n\nMoving to military news, the Pentagon is investigating suspected cyberattacks on freezers at six military grocery stores. The incidents took place in August 2026 at military commissaries, which sell tax-free goods to service members. Hackers allegedly altered freezer temperatures, causing food inside the stores to spoil. Fort Huachuca in Arizona reported a freezer entered defrost mode and heated up all its stored food. A Department of Defense official said US Cyber Command is working with law enforcement to investigate. Military investigators are also working with French authorities, who faced supermarket freezer failures in October. Officials have not established a direct link between the United States incidents and those in France. Investigators have not yet publicly identified any suspects or explained how hackers accessed the systems.\n\nFinally, India has approved 22 billion dollars in military spending. This new spending occurs amid ongoing border tensions with China. Defense manufacturers from three foreign nations are likely to find substantial opportunities in the country. Those supplier nations include the United States, Israel, and Russia. These opportunities stem directly from India's expanding defense market.\n\nUp next, Microsoft plans to raise 25 billion dollars in debt for its artificial intelligence expansion.\n\nMicrosoft plans to raise 25 billion dollars in bonds to expand its infrastructure. The company holds a top-tier AAA credit rating from S and P. It is targeting 170 billion dollars in capital spending for 2026. Microsoft wants to meet demand from artificial intelligence labs like OpenAI and Anthropic. The company says money raised will fund working capital, spending, and debt repayment. Finance chief Amy Hood said Microsoft will access funding when market conditions are attractive. Other tech companies like Amazon and Alphabet have also engaged the bond market recently. The company says the debt offering should close on November 4.\n\nNext, Intel shares fell more than 20 percent after its sales forecast missed expectations. The computer chip maker expects fourth quarter revenue between 15 billion and 16 billion dollars. Wall Street analysts had expected 17 billion dollars. Intel also forecast adjusted earnings of 25 cents a share instead of 43 cents. A shortage of memory chips is driving up prices and cutting personal computer demand. Chief executive Lip-Bu Tan predicts memory pricing will be a severe issue in 2027. Intel expects the personal computer market to shrink 12 percent this year. Tan also cited raw material shortages and a factory shutdown in Israel.\n\nIn national security news, hackers have leaked personal data belonging to thousands of federal workers. A group calling themselves Distributed Denial of Secrets released information on over 40,000 Homeland Security employees. Another group called the Department of Peace released data on more than 41,000 staff members. The leak includes names, phone numbers, email addresses, and office locations across various states. The files expose immigration agents, operatives, and contractors. Some exposed information belongs to officials working in surveillance and intelligence. Distributed Denial of Secrets claimed the leak was fair because the government lacks transparency. The Department of Homeland Security is currently working to secure its systems.\n\nTurning to the economy, new applications for United States unemployment benefits fell to 194,000 last week. The Labor Department reported this is the lowest level of claims since mid-July. First-time filings declined from a revised 195,000 in the previous week. The four-week moving average also eased down to 196,750. Jobless claims measure weekly layoffs and have stayed below 230,000 all year. They have remained at or below 200,000 for seven weeks in a row. Employers added an average of 68,000 jobs per month so far in 2026. Investors are waiting for the October employment report, scheduled for release on November 6.\n\nMoving to global environment talks, several nations face accusations of undermining science at biodiversity meetings. Diplomats at the United Nations COP17 summit in Armenia say four countries are frustrating progress. Delegates named Saudi Arabia, Russia, Argentina, and Brazil as the countries leading this push. The dispute focuses on two scientific assessments about overlapping nature and climate crises. Nearly 200 nations were supposed to agree on how to use these scientific findings. The fight centers on whether the text should welcome or merely take note of the reports. A French delegate described the current draft as a text without ambition. The summit is scheduled to end on October 30, with key scientific issues still unresolved.\n\nIn business news across Asia, two companies agreed to explore power solutions for data centres. Sembcorp Utilities and DayOne Data Centers signed a pact to study regional energy projects. The companies will evaluate on-site power generation, renewable energy, and maintenance services. DayOne runs an initiative that plans energy early to secure resources like power and water. The company already has renewable power agreements for data centers in Singapore, Malaysia, and Thailand. Sembcorp executive Koh Chiap Khiong said digital infrastructure requires energy infrastructure to grow alongside it. DayOne says it is targeting 100 percent renewable energy by 2030.\n\nBefore we go, here is your thought for the day.\n\nMany people do not just watch live streams anymore. They often leave them running like a constant hum in their homes. This habit is less about active engagement and more about ambient company. It creates a low-stakes sense of shared presence through a familiar voice in the background. What is interesting is how streaming platforms have quietly redefined alone time. They offer an on-demand sense of companionship. This dynamic blurs the line between public performance and private intimacy. We have begun outsourcing our quiet moments to digital friends. As digital companionship grows, how do you choose to spend your quiet moments?\n\nThank you for listening to the Sift Daily Briefing, and stay well until next time.",
